      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "The present study aims to overcome problems associated with the early diagnosis of allergic bronchopulmonary mycosis (ABPM) using the current criteria."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PATIENTS AND METHODS",
          "content": "Clinical features including radiographic findings from 10 patients with definitive ABPM based on the diagnostic criteria of Rosenberg-Patterson were compared with those from 9 patients with ABPM clinically diagnosed by respiratory allergy specialists."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "ABPM should be considered in patients with peripheral blood eosinophilia and pulmonary infiltration and/or central bronchiectasis when serum total IgE is elevated. Complication by bronchial asthma suggested ABPM, but was not essential. The expectoration of sputum containing solid components was a critical factor in patients with a history in ABPM. Evaluation of sputum cultures, serum specific IgE antibodies, skin tests and precipitating antibodies were required to establish a diagnosis, but the positive rate of these tests remained low."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Even when a definitive diagnosis cannot be established, systemic corticosteroid therapy should be initiated for clinically diagnosed ABPM to prevent irreversible pulmonary dysfunction."
        }
      ]},
